---
import ProductFooter from '@/components/ProductFooter.astro';
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';

// Static Astro landing overlaid at `/` during build:cf.
// The public preview stays a native GET form with no client-side JavaScript.
const TITLE = 'Starboard — project-aware tool discovery for GitHub';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Start with a public GitHub project, find similar repositories, and evaluate open-source tools with visible source evidence.';
---

<Layout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION}>
  <div class="site-shell">
    <header class="site-header">
      <a href="/" class="brand" aria-label="Starboard home">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">★</span>
        <span>
          <strong>Starboard</strong>
          <small>project-aware discovery</small>
        </span>
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href="/discover">Discover</a>
        <a href="/tools">Tool intelligence</a>
        <a href="/about">How it works</a>
        <a class="nav-sign-in" href="/login">Sign in</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="chart-kicker"><span>01</span> Project-aware GitHub discovery</p>
          <h1 id="hero-title">Find open-source tools that fit the project in front of you.</h1>
          <p class="hero-deck">
            Paste a public GitHub repository. Starboard finds similar projects, then shows which
            tools those peers use and the exact repository evidence behind each suggestion.
          </p>

          <form action="/project-preview" method="get" class="project-form">
            <label for="hero-repository">Public GitHub repository</label>
            <div>
              <input
                id="hero-repository"
                name="repository"
                required
                autocomplete="off"
                autocapitalize="none"
                spellcheck="false"
                placeholder="github.com/owner/repository"
              />
              <button type="submit">Preview project <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></button>
            </div>
          </form>

          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a href="/discover">Browse the public catalog</a>
            <span>Cataloged previews need no account</span>
          </div>
          <p class="boundary-note">
            Uncataloged repositories use your signed-in GitHub session. Nothing is saved until you
            explicitly connect a project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="route-chart" aria-label="How Starboard moves from a project to inspectable tool evidence">
          <div class="chart-heading">
            <span>Recommendation route</span>
            <span>Evidence remains attached</span>
          </div>
          <div class="route-start">
            <span class="route-index">01</span>
            <div>
              <small>Start with one repository</small>
              <strong>owner / project</strong>
              <p>Language · topics · metadata · detected tools</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <ol class="route-steps">
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div><strong>Build a peer set</strong><small>Retrieve broadly, then rerank with visible signals.</small></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div><strong>Trace tool support</strong><small>Only detections found in those peers can support a suggestion.</small></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div><strong>Inspect the source</strong><small>Open the peer repository and review the evidence yourself.</small></div>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="chart-caption"><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span> A route to investigate, not a claim that one tool is universally best.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="thesis" aria-labelledby="thesis-title">
        <div>
          <p class="chart-kicker"><span>02</span> Why project context matters</p>
          <h2 id="thesis-title">Popular is easy to find. Relevant takes context.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Trending lists answer what is attracting attention. Starboard asks a narrower question:
          what do projects like yours use, and what evidence supports taking a closer look?
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="evidence-section" aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
        <header class="section-heading">
          <p class="chart-kicker"><span>03</span> The evidence ledger</p>
          <h2 id="evidence-title">A recommendation you can audit.</h2>
          <p>Each layer narrows the search while keeping enough context for you to challenge the result.</p>
        </header>

        <div class="ledger">
          <article>
            <span class="ledger-number">A</span>
            <p class="ledger-label">Project context</p>
            <h3>Read the repository that is already in front of you.</h3>
            <p>Starboard uses visible language, topics, repository metadata, and detected tools. It does not claim access to private code.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span class="ledger-number">B</span>
            <p class="ledger-label">Grounded peers</p>
            <h3>Compare against similar public repositories.</h3>
            <p>Semantic, lexical, language, topic, and tool signals produce a bounded peer set. Sparse results are labeled as broader fallbacks.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span class="ledger-number">C</span>
            <p class="ledger-label">Tool support</p>
            <h3>See which peers supply the evidence.</h3>
            <p>Tool suggestions name their supporting repositories and detection confidence, with direct links for manual review.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <aside class="honesty-strip" aria-label="Recommendation limits">
          <strong>What Starboard does not claim</strong>
          <p>No universal best-tool ranking. No complete dependency audit. No benchmarked recommendation accuracy. The final judgment stays with you.</p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="modes" aria-labelledby="modes-title">
        <header class="section-heading compact">
          <p class="chart-kicker"><span>04</span> Two useful modes</p>
          <h2 id="modes-title">Explore in public. Add your context when it helps.</h2>
        </header>

        <div class="mode-grid">
          <article class="mode-card public-mode">
            <div class="mode-topline"><span>Public waters</span><strong>No account for cataloged projects</strong></div>
            <h3>Discover repositories and inspect tool evidence.</h3>
            <p>Search the seeded public catalog, open repository details, browse Tool Intelligence, and preview projects Starboard already knows.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Semantic and lexical repository discovery</li>
              <li>Language, tool, and growth filters</li>
              <li>Visible detection source and confidence</li>
            </ul>
            <a href="/discover">Explore Discover <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </article>

          <article class="mode-card personal-mode">
            <div class="mode-topline"><span>Personal workspace</span><strong>GitHub sign-in</strong></div>
            <h3>Connect a project or clean up a crowded star library.</h3>
            <p>Choose your own public projects for saved recommendations, then search and organize the repositories you have starred over time.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Saved public-project connections</li>
              <li>Lists, tags, notes, and starred-repository sync</li>
              <li>No GitHub write access</li>
            </ul>
            <a href="/login">Connect GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="access-section" aria-labelledby="access-title">
        <div class="access-intro">
          <p class="chart-kicker"><span>05</span> Access chart</p>
          <h2 id="access-title">Know what happens before you click.</h2>
          <p>Starboard is free today. Signing in adds your GitHub context; it does not unlock a paid tier.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="access-list">
          <div><dt>Browse Discover and tools</dt><dd><span class="status-dot public"></span>Public</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Preview a cataloged project</dt><dd><span class="status-dot public"></span>Public</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Look up an uncataloged project</dt><dd><span class="status-dot sign-in"></span>GitHub sign-in</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Save project recommendations</dt><dd><span class="status-dot sign-in"></span>GitHub sign-in</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Sync and organize your stars</dt><dd><span class="status-dot sign-in"></span>GitHub sign-in</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="final-cta" aria-labelledby="final-title">
        <p class="chart-kicker"><span>06</span> Set a course</p>
        <h2 id="final-title">Start with one public project.</h2>
        <p>See the peers, follow the evidence, and decide which tools deserve a closer look.</p>
        <div>
          <a class="primary-link" href="#hero-repository">Preview a project <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span></a>
          <a class="secondary-link" href="/discover">Browse Discover</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <ProductFooter />
  </div>
</Layout>
